rsx: tiles bound over main memory are tiled for the RSX's own accesses (NV3089 copies, the draw engine's guest reads
and writes).

Resistance 3 binds tile 4 over main memory (IO 0x30000, 0x580000 bytes, pitch 8192, bank 2). While aiming it
downsamples the scene colour and depth with NV3089 into it (two 480x352 images at 0x30000 and 0x30800), an SPU
depth-of-field job reads and writes them in the tiled layout the RSX gives them, and the RSX samples the job's output
as a texture. Copied in and read back linearly, the job's output landed on rows 0-15 of every 64 (a 1920-byte-wide
rectangle, tiled, covers exactly those linear rows) and showed as horizontal bands over the scene; the blur never
appeared at all.

- rsx_tiling.h (new, header only): the tiled address of a byte, a port of RPCS3's rsx::tiled_dma_copy (link and
  commit in the file), and a copy between a linear buffer and the tiling 32 bytes at a time (bits 0-4 carry over).
- cellGcmSetTile binds the tile, as RPCS3's does (tile.bound = pitch > 0): the game never calls BindTile. Set, Bind,
  Unbind and the resets pass the table to the engine (rsx_draw_engine_set_tile); only main-memory tiles count,
  local ones are transparent (RPCS3 skips them too).
- nv3089_blit: a source or destination in such a tile goes a row at a time through the tiling (gcm_tile_row; the
  stores notify SPU reservations as gcm_row_copy's do, now through gcm_store).
- rsx_draw_engine: texture uploads, guest_wrote and the surface seed read a tiled region through the tiling
  (eng_guest_linear); guest_read and the zeta store write through it. A tile whose IO pages are not contiguous in
  host memory is read and written linearly, as before (logged).
- Not tiled (TODO in rsx_tiling.h, RPCS3 does not either): NV309E copies from a tiled source, NV0039, vertex and
  index fetches.

test_rsx_tiling (tools/test_rsx_tiling.py): addresses for four pitch classes against a separate port of the RPCS3
function, the tiling permutes the tile's 32-byte blocks, a row round-trips. test_rsx_draw_engine: a target in a
tiled region is stored and loaded through the tiling, a texture there decodes the tiled bytes, an unbound tile is
linear again. test_nv3089_engine_read: 2:1 and 1:1 blits into a tile in main memory put every pixel at its tiled
address. The fixtures that compile cellGcmSys.c in (tests/test_gcm_io_alloc.c too) stub rsx_draw_engine_set_tile.
In the game (Haven street fight, aiming): no bands, the depth-of-field blur appears; a dump of the tile at the
texture read, detiled, is clean on every row. Cost: about 1.1 ms of CPU a frame while aiming (tiled copy and
texture read), nothing otherwise.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
